(also known as rescheduling) to update the initial schedule based on changes [7]. During these real-time events, in a rapidly changing market environment, the arrival of new orders may occur more frequently than ever before [8]. Due to the intrinsic characteristics of AM orders, which predominantly originate from individual customers, these orders demonstrate a high degree of heterogeneity and dynamic arrival patterns. Therefore, it is of utmost importance to devise a stable optimization method that can efficiently address the scheduling intricacies arising from the dynamic nature of orders and the dynamic AM environment. There are three significant challenges. (1) How to perform scheduling under a dynamic arrival pattern, ensuring efficient and timely allocation of resources? It is impractical to perform complete rescheduling every time a new order arrives. Therefore, leveraging historical data and gaining insights into patterns becomes essential for making well-informed scheduling decisions in the present context. (2) How to explore optimal order combinations within a limited AM machine area [7] to achieve cost-effectiveness and expedite manufacturing? Considering the high cost of AM machine acquisition and the significant processing time associated with AM orders, grouping AM parts, and allocating them to AM machines as different sets of part batches can improve both cost efficiency and manufacturing speed. Moreover, considering that the batch’s restriction condition is solely the area, the number of parts within a batch is not fixed, making it an arbitrary size batch processing problem. (3) How to effectively manage the randomly arriving customer orders that arise from a dynamic scheduling environment, in the context of order-machine allocation? Particularly, in the case of AM-based manufacturers with multiple heterogeneous AM machines, the nature of customer orders involves multi-variety and small-batch manufacturing. The capability of each AM machine is determined by factors such as its working area, achievable precision, and the availability of printable materials. Customized orders require specific machines that meet eligibility constraints which further complicates the dynamic process of dispatching orders to the appropriate machines. The scheduling problem in this parallel non-identical AM machine environment involves arbitrary size batch processing, release time considerations, and machine eligibility constraints, which can be represented as Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj . The primary objective we consider is to minimize the total tardiness time, ensuring the on-time delivery of customer orders. This problem falls under the category of NP-hard, as it generalizes the NP-hard scheduling problem with batch processing when the number of machines exceeds two [8]. Previous research on dynamic production scheduling in non-identical machine environments has focused on heuristic algorithms [9,10], operational research methods [11–14] and machine learning algorithms [15]. However, when dealing with randomly arriving orders in AM, a fast and efficient dynamic scheduling approach becomes necessary. Exact procedures have limitations in solving large-scale problems, while heuristic rules struggle to select the best approach at a specific time point. In the context of the Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj problem, accurately observing and modeling discrete uncertainty disturbances, such as new order arrivals, is challenging. In recent years, deep reinforcement learning (DRL) has achieved remarkable success in many real areas [16,17]. DRL methods have shown effectiveness in handling dynamic and complex states [18–20], including vehicle dispatch [21, 22], robot control [23], advertising resource allocation [24] and so forth. However, existing literature utilizing DRL to solve part-machine matching problems often focuses on simple assignment of a single order to a machine [25]. To address the complexity of dynamic batch scheduling, some studies divide the problem into two stages [15]. For example, they first select a part to add to a batch and then allocate the batch to a machine. However, this two-stage approach may not
effectively solve the Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj , especially when machine eligibility constraints are involved. Batches formed in the first stage may not find a suitable machine during the second stage, resulting in reduced scheduling efficiency. This study addresses the Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj problem and proposes an innovative approach called the out-of-order enabled dueling deep Q network (O3-DDQN). We describe the problem using a mixed integer programming (MIP) model and formulate it as a Markov Decision Process (MDP). To capture valuable real-time information, a dynamic ‘look around’ method is introduced, extracting threedimensional features of orders, machines, and objectives. In addition, this study introduces five composite rules based on the out-of-order principle, specifically designed to effectively handle complex production constraints in batching and order-machine matching decisions. To evaluate the agent’s actions, a reward function closely aligned with the objective is specifically designed to provide meaningful feedback. At last, numerical experiments are conducted to demonstrate the superiority of the O3-DDQN model over other approaches. This innovative approach provides a practical solution for the scheduling challenges in AM. The paper is organized as follows. Section 2 provides the literature review. After providing a detailed description and formulation of the Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj in Section 3, Section 4 gives the implementation details of the proposed O3-DDQN. The results of numerical experiments are presented in Section 5. Finally, conclusions with future directions are drawn in Section 6.
2. Literature review
2.1. Planning and scheduling in AM
The global AM products and services market experienced an overall growth rate of 18.3% and is expected to reach an impressive US$18 billion according to Wohlers Report 2022 [26]. This growth indicates that AM has the potential to revolutionize various industries such as aerospace, automotive, and medical fields [27]. The increasing demand of production through AM poses challenges to efficient AM machine scheduling. However, there has been relatively little research focused on the production planning and scheduling processes in the AM industry [28]. Traditional manufacturing scheduling methods cannot be directly applied to AM [2],where the processing time is typically determined by the component with the longest processing time or the sum of processing times for all components in a batch [29], AM-based production involves variable processing times. In AM, the batch processing time is dependent on the specific components being produced, including factors such as the total volume of allocated components and, importantly, the maximum height within the batch [12]. The introduction of batch processing in AM aims to reduce overall processing time. However, due to various constraints such as machine capacity, maximum supported height, and supported materials, certain parts may not be eligible for allocation on specific machines. As a result, determining the optimal combination of parts to be produced on each machine becomes challenging. Current studies on AM planning and scheduling can be broadly categorized as static and dynamic approaches. In previous studies on static scheduling in the AM domain, Li et al. [1] aimed to minimize production costs by addressing the planning and scheduling problem in additive manufacturing. They proposed a mathematical model and introduced two heuristic procedures, namely the ‘best-fit’ and ‘adapted best-fit’ rules. Chergui et al. focused on the planning, nesting, and scheduling problems in AM, aiming to fulfill customer orders within specified due dates. The researchers proposed a heuristic approach and conducted experimental tests that highlighted the importance of planning and scheduling for optimized production in AM [30]. Zhang et al. tackled the challenging 3D-printing shop scheduling problem in AM. They proposed an improved evolutionary algorithm that combined a genetic algorithm with a heuristic placement


