 incoming orders. To tackle this dynamic problem, they proposed a strategy-based metaheuristic decision-making approach [29]. Luo et al. focused on a dynamic and static data-based matching method for cloud 3D printing, leveraging the cloud manufacturing paradigm. They proposed a modeling framework to describe print tasks and resources, facilitating efficient matching. Their method provided a technical solution for quick supply–demand matching of large-scale resources in the cloud manufacturing environment. It considered both the static and dynamic characteristics of 3D printers, enabling real-time monitoring and automatic diagnosis of printer health status [33]. The existing literature primarily focuses on heuristic algorithms for solving the AM scheduling problem. However, the dynamic arrival of orders and the diverse requirements of these orders make the problem more complex. To address this, a research gap exists in the application of DRL methods. DRL has the advantage of learning optimal strategies directly from historical data and interactions with the environment. Moreover, DRL is well-suited for dynamic and changing environments, which heuristic algorithms are not able to handle effectively.
2.2. DRL in dynamic scheduling problem with batch processing
Currently, there is limited existing literature that specifically explores the utilization of DRL methods to address the dynamic scheduling problem in AM. Modeling of the environment’s dynamics is not necessary for DRL agent to learn good policies. Hence, it can be wellsuited when the environment changes over time [34–36]. However, we can draw insights from other production environments where DRL has proven effective in solving batch scheduling problems. In the existing literature focusing on batch processing in production, two fundamental factors significantly influence the outcomes of batching and scheduling: the allocation of different parts to create batches and the efficient matching of these batches with the appropriate machines. The way parts are grouped together in a batch can have a substantial impact on product delivery times [37–39]. It is crucial to recognize the
importance of optimizing the combination of multiple parts produced within the same tray, considering the limitations of available space [7]. In semiconductor manufacturing, Kong et al. introduced a DQN algorithm to address the single batch-processing machine problem [40] for saving energy purpose. Considering technological constraints, logistic constraints, and production quantity, Waschneck et al. applied reinforcement learning with the DQN agent to production scheduling [41]. Zhang et al. introduced a simulation-based neural fitted Q learning to build a decision maker (1) whether to start processing a batch or to wait for more jobs joining the batch, and (2) which batch should be processed first in a real-time job shop. Zhu et al. considered a flow jobshop scheduling problem with combined processing constraints and virtual operation. They proposed a reinforcement learning method for the problem which is used to model the scheduling process. The results indicated that the performance of the proposed method is significantly better than those of the common dispatching rules [42]. Wang et al. investigated a two-stage hybrid flow shop scheduling problem with batch machines and jobs arriving over time. The research proposed an independent double deep-q-network (DQN)-based multiagent reinforcement learning approach to produce an adaptive rule for batch forming and scheduling [16]. Cals et al. proposed a DRL approach combined with a heuristic to determine the best way to batch and pick orders, minimizing delays. The DRL solution is trained using a state representation and proximal policy optimization algorithm [43]. However, the most existing literature that utilizes DRL to tackle the complexity of dynamic batch scheduling divides the problem into two stages: select a part to add to a batch and then allocate the batch to a machine. However, this strategy may not effectively address the Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj problem, especially when machine eligibility constraints are involved. This increases the complexity and computational resources required for training, potentially leading to inefficiencies. Additionally, batches formed in the first stage may struggle to find a suitable machine during the second stage, leading to reduced scheduling efficiency. We aim to develop a method to tackle the limitations of the two-stage method in scheduling the highly personalized and dynamic orders. Only one network needs to be trained to simultaneously decide group batches and machine allocation, whereas the two-stage approach requires at least two networks. In general, the literature describes training a single network to decide batch formation and machine assignment during the first stage. This approach is efficient when the differences between parts are minimal. However, in an AM production environment, where orders mostly come from different customers with high degrees of customization and personalization, this can lead to inefficiencies. Specifically, batches combined in the first stage may not find a compatible machine for processing in the second stage, resulting in wasted time and reduced overall efficiency.
3. Problem description and formulation
3.1. Problem description
In this section, we delve into the Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj problem. This problem involves a set of AM machines and a set of customer orders, which are received randomly with an average time interval. Each order j has a release time rj and a due date dj . When a customer order arrives, it is added to the current orders pool. Subsequently, the orders are regrouped into different batches and allocated to suitable machines, as shown in Fig. 1. It is important to note that the batches processed on machine i should not exceed the machine’s capacity, including factors such as the machine’s physical capacity (i.e. height, area, and precision). Unlike conventional batch scheduling problems with fixed processing time, the batch processing time in this problem is variable. It depends on the characteristics of the orders, such as the total volume of the allocated


