4. Proposed methods
The Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj problem requires an innovative intelligent mechanism to handle the challenges of mass customization. To address the complex production constraints and dynamic needs in AM production, a DRL-based method is proposed. In this section, we outline the key modules of the proposed O3-DDQN approach, including state features, out-of-order-based scheduling rule actions, reward functions, network structure, and training framework. By combining these
components, our approach aims to provide an effective solution for dynamic AM scheduling in mass customization, optimizing tardiness, and ensuring customer satisfaction. The state features capture the current status of the production environment, encompassing order-related information, machine characteristics, and metrics reflecting scheduling delays. The actions involve selecting scheduling rules based on the current state to optimize production efficiency. Reward functions provide feedback to balance multiple objectives, guiding the agent towards optimal policies. Our network structure includes mechanisms like Double Q-learning to reduce bias, and the training framework uses experience replay to enhance learning efficiency and stability. The following subsections will provide a detailed introduction to these key components.
4.1. The scheduling environment
We have developed an integrated agent for dynamic AM batch and matching, which effectively combines orders and allocates AM machines while considering machine capacity and eligibility constraints. To facilitate the agent’s interaction with the AM scheduling environment and update parameters, we utilize the dueling Deep Q-Network (DDQN) architecture, as illustrated in Fig. 2. The scheduling problem can be modeled as a Markov Decision Process (MDP), where the probability distribution of the next scheduling state depends solely on the current time step. In this MDP, we define a tuple (S, A, R, P ), where S represents the state space and A represents the action space. As actions are executed, the MDP evolves, and the agent receives rewards r ∈ R based on the reward function (R), obtaining the next state from the updated MDP. The DRL algorithm learns the state value function through a neural network without explicitly relying on explicitly designing the state transition probability.
4.1.1. State spaces
There is a consensus among both researchers and practitioners that smart manufacturing involves digitizing factory resources (human, machines, materials, facilities/assets, etc.) into digital data using smart sensors, thereby creating digital production systems [44]. According to Fu et al. [45], this process includes technological enhancements such as advanced manufacturing facilities, equipment, and smart IoT devices, as well as theoretical extensions of existing decision models to handle unstructured data generated and collected from distributed sensors in


