4.1.2. Action spaces
The processing of orders on AM machines is influenced by the availability of resources such as materials and machines. The DRL agent efficiently manages parallel non-identical AM machines that share a single order queue and operate based on the out-of-order principle. This principle, inspired by modern computer central processing units, aims to improve performance bottlenecks and enhance resource utilization based on available resources and data dependencies [46–49]. The core concept of the out-of-order principle is to swiftly match orders with AM machines, considering real-time resource availability, particularly idle production windows and operational constraints (e.g., accuracy, material, height). This approach minimizes production pauses caused by mismatches in processing. Unlike in-order scheduling rules, scheduling rules based on the out-of-order principle allow processors to execute subsequent orders without waiting, as illustrated in Fig. 4. To tackle the scheduling problem Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj , we employ out-of-order-based scheduling rules to address stalls and improve processing efficiency. In the context of AM, this entails the ability to skip certain AM orders in the queue that do not meet the processing requirements, allowing other orders to proceed. This flexible approach enhances production adaptability, especially when dealing with dynamic order arrivals. Assuming we have a set of idle AM machines and a sequenced order pool, the batch and allocation decision-making process based on the out-of-order principle is outlined in Algorithm 3. This algorithm utilizes the out-of-order principle to determine the optimal batch and allocation decisions.
