4.2. Structure and implementation of O3-DDQN
4.2.1. DRL-based algorithm: DQN and extension to dueling DQN
DQN is a reinforcement learning algorithm that combines DL and Q learning. It uses a deep neural network to approximate the Q function and select the optimal action by maximizing the action value function.
DQN solves some problems in traditional Q-learning, such as too large a state space and difficulty in generalization. DQN is also widely used in the field of optimization problems [16,22,51]. Dueling DQN (DDQN) is an improved version of DQN, which introduces an important concept: dueling architecture [52]. Fig. 1 gives an illustration of the proposed DDQN. The improvement makes DDQN perform better than the original DQN in our optimization problem. In dueling architecture, the Q value function is decomposed into two parts. One part is the state value function V , which represents the expected return in a certain state; the other part is the advantage function A, which represents the advantage of choosing a certain heuristic action relative to the average effect in a certain state. This decomposition allows the network to learn state values and advantage values separately to better understand the AM environment. As a DRL-based algorithm, DDQN can be a powerful tool for optimizing the scheduling process in AM, leading to more reliable and stable scheduling decisions. Compared to DQN, DDQN also has advantages in improving Efficiency and enhancing adaptability by finding better scheduling actions more efficiently and better adapting to dynamic changes in the AM environment.
4.2.2. Overall training framework of the O3-DDQN
In the training process, the rescheduling point t is defined as every time a new order arrives or an AM machine has just become idle. The overall training framework of the proposed O3-DDQN is provided in Algorithm 9. At each rescheduling point t, the O3-DDQN takes the state features st as input and selects a dispatching rule at at which chooses several unprocessed orders, then groups them into a batch and assigns it on a feasible AM machine. Here, the state features st are obtained by the previously mentioned ‘‘look around’’ method. By observing from multiple directions, the ‘‘look around’’ method aims to establish the necessary infrastructure for real-time visibility and traceability, enabling the accurate selection of OoO-based rules, that is, at. After executing at, the system enters a new rescheduling point t + 1 and the immediate reward rt can be calculated based on the chosen action. A priority experience replay buffers are used to store the transitions of the DDQN agent. To mitigate the overestimation bias inherent in Q-learning, O3-DDQN uses Double Q-learning. This involves maintaining two separate Q-networks to decouple action selection from Q-value estimation, resulting in more accurate value predictions. We use historical data to train the model which is stored in the priority replay memory. Prioritized experience replay, compared to normal experience replay, adds a sampling priority to each experience in the original replay buffer to make sampling more efficient. In order to balance the exploration and exploitation, we adopt an ε-greedy action selection policy in the training process.
4.2.3. Network structure
The proposed O3-DDQN agent uses deep neural network (DNN) as the Q-function approximator. The DL network consists of five fully connected layers with one input layer, one output layer and three hidden layers. The numbers of nodes belonging to the input and output layers are the same as the numbers of state features and available actions, respectively. The number of nodes in each hidden layer is 256. We use the }ReLU ′ activation function for the input, output, and hidden layers.
