5.1. Parameter settings and training process
The training and testing benchmarks used in this paper are generated at random. The proposed O3-DDQN is implemented on a PC with i5-13600kf @4.95 GHz and 64 GB RAM, Nvidia GeForce RTX 3060 TI. The parameters of the training algorithm are elaborately set by preliminary experiments as shown in Table 2. In the training process, a new instance is randomly generated at each epoch to improve the generality of O3-DDQN in different production environments. Each instance used in this study is generated according to the parameters listed in Table 3. At each training episode, the ε-greedy parameter ε is linearly decreased from 0.9 to 0.05 per training step so that each rule can be adequately investigated during the training process. Therefore, the algorithm can avoid falling into the local optimum. To assess the effectiveness of the agent’s batch forming and scheduling strategies through interactive data and behavior analysis, we conducted training in a simulation environment. The parameter settings of the training method and instance generation are listed in Tables 2 and 3. The agent actively engaged with a Markov decision process, facilitating interaction and information transfer, and then underwent end-to-end training within its own framework, utilizing Algorithm 9 to batch sample data from the replay memory. Upon completion of the training, we evaluated the agent’s performance by analyzing metrics
such as loss value, reward, and total tardiness time obtained by the O3-DDQN algorithm in each episode. Fig. 5 demonstrates a consistent decrease in the agent’s loss at every episode, highlighting effective learning throughout the training process. Fig. 6 illustrates the variation of reward. The initial exploration rate is 1, allowing the agent to explore as many samples as possible. As more samples are added, the exploration rate gradually decreases. During this process, the exploration rate decreases by 0.001 for each completed episode until it stabilizes at 0.05. From the trend of reward, it can be found that before episode roughly 700, when the exploration rate is greater than 0.3, the reward obtained by the agent does not rise significantly but explores the highest reward value throughout the whole process. in the case where the exploration rate gradually reduced to below 0.3, i.e., after the 700th episode, the value of reward increased significantly, indicating that both the reward function design and the network design are reasonable and effective, and are able to learn the action of reducing the total tardiness time in dynamic and complex AM environmental interactions. Additionally, Fig. 7 depicts a decline in the trend of total tardiness time as the number of training episodes increases. The trend in the total tardiness time is the opposite of the trend in reward. This is predictable because the value of forward is a negative multiple of the value of the total tardiness time. These observations indicate that the proposed O3-DDQN algorithm gradually learns the appropriate dispatching rules for different scenarios in an efficient manner. Through interactive data and behavior analysis, we have verified the effectiveness of the agent’s batch forming and scheduling strategies, demonstrating its ability to adapt and optimize its performance in complex and dynamic situations.
