6. Conclusion
In this paper, we proposed the O3-DDQN method to address the Rm ∣ batch {AM}, rj , Mj ∣ ∑ Tj problem, aiming to minimize total tardiness through batch formation and scheduling. The study highlights the importance of considering the dynamic nature of order arrivals and the diverse characteristics of AM orders. It emphasizes the need for efficient scheduling decisions that ensure timely resource allocation. Through the utilization of historical data and pattern insights, the proposed O3-DDQN approach enables informed scheduling decisions, enhancing production efficiency. Considering the need for the limitations of single heuristic scheduling rules, we introduced the O3-DDQN approach to enable adaptive selection of five composite scheduling rules which based on out-of-order principle. The O3-DDQN agent is developed to estimate the state–action value for each rule, enabling the selection of the most suitable dispatching rules at each rescheduling point. This approach enhances scheduling efficiency and effectively manages the complexities arising from a dynamic environment. Our experimental results demonstrated the effectiveness of the O3DDQN method, showcasing its superiority over composite scheduling rules in all instances. The average improvement rate of performance reaches 13.09%. Furthermore, compared to the classic O3-DQN agent with the same state space, action space, and reward function, the O3DDQN method achieved a remarkable improvement rate of 6.54%. This research provides valuable insights and a practical framework for the efficient scheduling of dynamic orders in a real-time AM environment. The proposed O3-DDQN approach, with its integration of out-of-orderbased composite rules and DRL, demonstrates its potential for achieving improved overall performance and addressing the challenges faced by AM-based manufacturers engaged in mass customization. The consistent outperformance of O3-DDQN over single composite scheduling rules, randomly chosen rules, and the classic O3-DQN demonstrates its robust capability to learn and implement effective dispatching strategies. This underscores the critical importance of adopting adaptive scheduling systems that can dynamically respond to changing conditions, such as urgent due dates or frequent new order arrivals. The flexibility of O3-DDQN positions it as an invaluable tool for managing diverse and dynamic production scenarios. Our approach optimizes the allocation of resources by ensuring that tasks are prioritized and scheduled in a manner that maximizes the utilization of available assets. This optimization can result in significant cost savings and enhanced productivity within the manufacturing environment. Therefore, AM manufacturers should consider integrating such adaptive algorithms to optimize scheduling processes and improve operational efficiency. In future work, we plan to consider additional uncertain disturbances, such as machine breakdowns, to enhance the robustness of the scheduling system. We also intend to explore the inclusion of the twodimensional batching problem when combining AM batches, further optimizing the scheduling process. Additionally, as the O3-DDQN is a value-based method and cannot directly optimize the agent’s policy, we will investigate other state-of-the-art model-based reinforcement learning methods to further improve performance.
